N2  - Purpose: Complete abstinence from pregnancy is recommended for women supported on durable left ventricular assist devices (LVAD). However, the safety and efficacy of hormonal contraception strategies in women on LVADs are not well described. Methods: A retrospective cohort of all female patients aged 50 or younger supported with HeartMate II (HM2), HeartMate 3 (HM3), or HeartWare (HW) devices from two LVAD centers in the United States was evaluated for contraceptive strategies and prior history of hysterectomy/tubal ligation. Contraceptive failure and adverse events (LVAD-associated hemolysis or thrombosis and venous thromboembolic events [VTE]) were assessed. Results: Thirty-one females (age: 37 ± 9 years, weight: 86 ± 23 kg, and BMI: 32 ± 8 kg/m2) were included with an average support duration of 34 months. Patients were supported with HM2 (4 [13%]); HW (15 [49%]); and HM3 (12 [39%]) devices and were primarily bridge to transplant (17 [55%]). Relevant comorbidities included type 2 diabetes mellitus (4 [13%]), systemic hypertension (16 [52%]), PCOS (2 [6%]), and prior tobacco use (2 [6%]). On average, patients had 3.3 ± 2.7 prior pregnancies and 1.7 ± 1.7 children prior to LVAD. In the cohort, 17 (55%) used contraception (10 IUD, 5 oral contraceptive pills, 2 hormonal contraceptive implants), 8 (26%) had a prior tubal ligation or hysterectomy, 5 (16%) used no form of contraception, and 1 had ovarian failure related to chemotherapy. Pregnancy occurred in 2 (6%) patients (1 IUD, 1 no contraception, 0.02 pregnancies per patient-year). LVAD thrombotic complications occurred in 4 (17%) patients, only one of whom was in the hormonal contraception group. Thus, contraceptive use was not associated with LVAD hemolysis or thrombosis (OR 0.22, 95% CI 0.02-2.53). However, VTE events were noted in 3 (10%) patients, all of whom were on hormonal contraception (1 DVT on medroxyprogesterone, 1 DVT on IUD, and, 1 PE on norethindrone, 0.03 VTE events per patient-year). Conclusion: This study represents the first multicenter study evaluating contraceptive usage and adverse events in women on LVADs. These data suggest hormonal contraceptive therapy is associated with a low rate of LVAD-related thrombotic complications but with a higher rate of VTE events, suggesting the importance of enhanced vigilance. Contraception continues to be strongly advised on LVAD particularly as legal challenges to abortion are expanding.
