N2  - Background/Aims Patients with chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) are at high risk of thromboembolic events (TEA), which are a significant cause of morbidity and mortality. They are attributed to a pre-thrombotic state induced by the inflammatory activity of this disease. The aim of our study was to evaluate the prevalence and search for associated factors that may favor the occurrence of TTE during IBD. Methods This is a cross-sectional and descriptive study spread over a period of 5 years between July 2017 and August 2022, carried out in the gastroenterology department of the Ibn Rochd University Hospital of Casablanca, including all IBD patients who presented a venous thrombosis during their follow-ups. Only thromboembolic events confirmed by imaging were considered Results During the study period, we included 437 patients, of whom 9 had venous thrombosis, i.e., a prevalence of 2.05%. They were five women and four men, with a mean age of 46.3 +/- 9.6 years. Six patients had Crohn's disease, and three had ulcerative colitis. The mean time between the diagnosis of the disease and the occurrence of thrombosis was 41 months. It was a deep thrombosis of the lower limbs in five cases, of the portal vein in one case, of the spleno-mesenteric trunk in one case, a pulmonary embolism in one case, and a cerebral thrombophlebitis in one case.TEA was noted on admission in 4 patients (44.4%) or during hospitalization in 5 patients (55.6%). Six patients were on heparinoprophylaxis at the time of the thrombosis. In the context of the search for factors favoring thrombosis, at the time of the thromboembolic complication, all the patients were in a progressive phase of their disease, judged to be moderate in 6 cases and severe in 3 cases, the notion of prolonged immobilization was found in 3 patients, cholecystectomy in 2 patients, smoking in 2 patients, diabetes in 2 patients, and dyslipidemia in one patient.On the other hand, there was no obesity or hypertension among our patients. In the five female patients, there was no history of pregnancy, childbirth or oral contraception.The blood count was normal in all patients. The thrombophilia workup, including the determination of TA III, proteins C and S, factor V and II Leiden mutation, and anti-phospholipid antibodies was performed in 3 patients and was normal.The evolution was favorable in all patients under anticoagulant treatment. Conclusion The prevalence of venous thrombosis during IBD in our series was 2.05%. Venous thrombosis of the lower limbs was the most frequent location.These TEA seem to occur during the active phase of the disease, underscoring the importance of prevention of thromboembolic disease during relapses and also of insisting on smoking cessation.
