N2  - INTRODUCTION: Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MTB) is a rare infectious organism in developed countries. Miliary TB is an even rarer manifestation that is fatal when left untreated, and is characterized by lymphohematogenous spread of MTB. It likely accounts for less than 2% of all MTB infections. Miliary TB, let alone MTB infections in totality, is increasingly rare in developed countries in which TB is not considered an endemic organism. CASE: An 82-year-old Caucasian female with a history of chronic kidney disease, iron deficiency, and recurrent DVT of unknown provocation presented with 6 weeks of fatigue, night sweats, and dyspnea. She was hemodynamically stable upon arrival and maintained on room air. Chest x-ray (CXR) revealed diffuse nodular consolidations, which prompted computed tomogram (CT) scan showing innumerable pulmonary nodules with perilymphatic distribution and calcified mediastinal and hilar lymph nodes (Figure 1). She was admitted for workup and pulmonary and infectious disease consultation, with differential diagnosis of disseminated fungal or bacterial infection, sarcoidosis, interstitial lung disease, or rheumatologic disease. She was a retired medical assistant, up-to-date on tuberculosis screening with yearly CXR and tuberculin skin tests, and had no travel history to countries with endemic TB. Rheumatologic workup including ANA, RF, CCP were negative. HIV screening was negative, as were COVID-19 and respiratory viral panel. Serial induced sputums initially were negative for acid-fast bacilli (AFB), so she underwent bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) and transbronchial biopsies (TBBx). BAL sample was negative for AFB culture and smear, as was TTBx histopathology. The remainder of fungal and bacterial antigens (serum/urine) and BAL cultures were negative, including histoplasmosis, coccidioides, aspergillus, and blastomyces. After 3 weeks, initial induced sputums returned positive for AFB; TBBx showed necrotizing granulomatous infection. RIF/NAAT for MTB complex returned positive. She was started on standard quadruple therapy for active tuberculosis. Unfortunately she suffered cardiac arrest after presenting to the emergency department with dyspnea few weeks after initiation of therapy and expired. DISCUSSION: Apart from exposure to endemic regions, predisposing factors for miliary TB include malnutrition, alcoholism, chronic kidney disease, post-gastrectomy status, organ transplantation, immunosuppression, connective tissue disorders, pregnancy, underlying malignancy, and silicosis. Additionally, diabetes mellitus and smoking have emerged as correlates. This case report underscores the importance of maintaining a high index of suspicion for miliary TB even in non-endemic countries, as prompt diagnosis is integral in reducing morbidity and mortality.
