N2  - Introduction: Breast cancer (BC) is a serious health issue that causes a large amount of morbidity and mortality. Despite the fact that signs and symptoms are properly coded, there may be unusual clinical presentations such as thromboembolic events, which frequently involve the upper limbs, particularly in diabetic individuals. Objective: The goal of this study is to look at the number of diabetic patients with undetected breast cancer who were initially diagnosed with upper extremities deep vein thrombosis and their risk of recurrence. Method: We retrospectively examined a seven years medical records of patients with breast cancer from the registry of the cardio-oncology unit of Casablanca. Thirty-seven patients presented for an initial diagnosis of upper-limb deep vein thrombosis with non-diagnosed breast cancer. No previous positive personal or family history of venous thromboembolism was reported. Questions about acquired risk factors, such as surgery, plaster cast immobilization, injury, unusual exercise and travel for more than 6 hours were excluded from the analysis for a time window of 3 months prior to the diagnosis date. Subjects on oral contraceptives, pregnancy and puerperium were also ruled out. Results: Thirty-seven patients (0 M – 37 F; median age 68 years; age range 46–77) reported at the time painful upper extremities swelling. The venous Doppler ultrasound examination showed thrombosis in the axillary vein (41%), subclavian vein (37.3%), brachial vein (14.9%) and cephalic vein (6.8%). Two cases (7.4%) also had a pulmonary embolism. Twenty-one patients (56.7%) had recurrent deep vein thrombosis over a mean follow-up of 4.6 years. Breast examination revealed signs of malignancy in 10.8% of cases. BC was confirmed clinically and instrumentally and distributed as follows: stage III (40.6%), stage IV (27.3%), stage II (21.6%) and stage I (10.5%). Twenty-nine patients were hormone receptor-positive in this study. Diabetes was linked to an increased risk of upper-limb deep vein thrombosis in underdiagnosed BC (OR 3.17; 95% CI, 1.01–9.80; P = 0.045) and its recurrence (OR 2.17; 95% CI, 0.69–5.31; P = 0.006). Conclusion: Upper-limb deep vein thrombosis is a typical initial presentation of breast cancer in elderly diabetic women, and if recognized promptly, may allow for an early diagnosis of malignancy. This subgroup should also be closely monitored for venous thromboembolism recurrence.
