N2  - Well-controlled Systemic Lupus Erythematosus(SLE) and lupus nephritis during pregnancy can end favorably, while the poorly controlled disease is known to lead to poor outcomes. A 25-year-old female with a healthy 6-year-old daughter treated with SLE for 1.5 years, complained of worsening lower extremity edema and eventually diagnosed with class V membranous lupus nephritis. She was maintained on Prednisone and Hydroxychloroquine(HCQ) while mycophenolate mofetil(MMF) was added on as a steroid-sparing agent. While she was compliant, her proteinuria decreased to 1.5 g per day. However, due to noncompliance, her proteinuria rapidly increased to the nephrotic range. Her uncontrolled lupus nephritis course further complicated when she was found to be eight weeks pregnant. During her pregnancy, she was on prednisone, AZA, and Tacrolimus and followed closely by high-risk obstetrics, nephrology, and rheumatology. In this period, proteinuria remained around 8-9 g /day for several weeks, and she was placed on Lovenox for Deep Venous Thrombosis prophylaxis along with Bactrim. She had sporadic compliance throughout pregnancy, which was complicated by gestational hypertension and gestational diabetes mellitus. At 35 weeks of gestation in the setting of preeclampsia, she underwent a cesarean section giving birth to a healthy male infant. In the postpartum period, she was compliant with her medications and remained on Tacrolimus, AZA, and a tapering dose of prednisone. Her proteinuria improved, and she continues to have excellent renal function. During the pregnancy, this patient experienced poorly controlled lupus nephritis with significant proteinuria. However, with close monitoring, she had a successful pregnancy and postpartum, she was able to regain good renal function. Active lupus nephritis does not necessarily preclude a successful outcome in pregnancy, provided that there is close coordinated care.
