N2  - Physiologic changes of pregnancy and cystic ﬁbrosis pathology provide a unique set of circumstances. Pulmonary disease accounts for over 90% of the morbidity and mortality of patients with cystic ﬁbrosis. These abnormalities create anesthetic challenges due to multiple organ systems being aﬀected including the respiratory, gastrointestinal, cardiovascular, and genitourinary tracts, where patients present with chronic respiratory failure, pancreatic insuﬃciency, poor nutrition, and cardiac manifestations. We present the perianesthetic management of a parturient with cystic ﬁbrosis who successfully underwent preterm cesarean delivery under neuraxial anesthesia with preemptive bilateral femoral venous sheaths placed for potential extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) initiation.
