N2  - Objective/Purpose Statement: Multiple factors have been proposed as the key determinants of successful closure and sustained venous occlusion following Endovenous Laser Ablation (EVLA). These include delivered energy, laser emission wavelength, pull back rate, continuous versus pulsed mode laser emission, type of fiber tip (bare, jacketed, radial) and the LEED (Linear Endovenous Energy Density). LEED is dependent on many factors including the power output (wattage=Joules/sec), treatment duration, and the rate/direction in which any given vein wall segment is exposed to thermal energy (pull back rate, beam geometry, laser fiber tip factors). Using a jacket-tip proprietary “non-vein wall contact” glass weld 600 mm fiber (effective 905 mm diameter, Angiodynamics Never TouchTM) in a large prospectively studied cohort of chronic venous insufficiency patients with clinical EVLA indications, the following objectives were studied in long term follow-up of at least 3months-1year: 1. Examine the key determinants of successful vein closure and laser-tissue interaction; more specifically, discern the relative importance of laser power output, LEED, and pull back rate in determining successful maintained vein occlusion across time. 2. Determine the threshold LEED value for 90% success in achieving sustained vein occlusion in long term follow-up. 3. Determine the optimal power setting for the Never TouchTM EVLA fiber to achieve the 90% success across a range of manual pull back rates used in clinical practice. Method: A prospective study of 203 (2014-2016) serial out-patients with chronic venous insufficiency/varicose veins/venous reflux symptoms, CEAP classification C2-C6, in one or both legs. Pre-procedure and follow up (Table presented) standing/supine duplex ultrasounds: patency, obstruction, and reflux in the GSV, SSV perforating and deep veins of both limbs. Reflux defined as >0.5 seconds reflux after releasing thigh or calf compression in the standing position. Exclusion criteria: prior deep vein thrombosis, age <18 years, lack of informed consent, pregnancy, inability to ambulate, current anticoagulant therapy, severely reduced pedal pulses, sclerosant allergy, prior vein ablation, or anatomic factors preventing catheter advancement. Tumescent anesthesia and EVLA power settings 6W-12W were utilized. Success Definition: Sustained occlusion of treated vein at any time point beyond one week after EVLA. Regression modeling utilized to determine predictors of success, LEED threshold, and optimal power settings. Results: 203 patients (59% female, 41% male) comprising 336 ablated veins were studied. 86% were greater (long) saphenous veins (GSV) and 14% were lesser (short) saphenous veins (SSV). No patients were lost to follow up or withdrew from the study. The mean reflux times in seconds for right GSV, right SSV and left GSV, left SSV were (3.8, 3.6) and (3.5, 3.5) respectively. Mean GSV diameter treated was 5.9mm (right GSV) and 6mm (left GSV). Mean age was 65.7 years; mean BMI was 31.8; mean was LEED 21.6 J/cm; mean pull back rate was 4.7 mm/sec; and mean power was 9.7 watts. Concomitant prevalence of cardiovascular risk factors was extensive: Diabetes (38.1%), hypertension (71.1%), hyperlipidemia (66%), chronic kidney disease (5.1%), and coronary artery disease (11.7%). Summary statistics are presented in Table 1. Figure 1 illustrates the frequency of success vs. failure of ablation as a function of power, LEED, and pull back rate. Based on these frequency distributions, we analyzed the power-to-success relationship by stratifying the power output into two groups (6-7 watts and 8-12 watts). The frequency of failure diminished markedly as the output increased at and above 8 watts. Maximal success:failure ratios were observed at the highest power outputs of 8 W-12W. The distributions of success to failure for the LEED relationship was skewed rightward and increased up to 20 J/cm and then decreased. Success exceeded failure at all pull back rates studied. Figure 2 presents the Kaplan-Meier curve for the analysis of the success time (in days) for the two power groups (6-7 w vs. 8-12 W). The Cox proportional hazards (PH) model indicated that the power group of 6-7W has decreased chance of success than 8-12W (estimate=1.1249; p=0.00079). This also implied that the power outputs from 8-12W were significantly better at maintaining successful long term closure after EVLT than 6-7W. We also examined the PH model with three predictors in the model: (1) power group (6-7 w vs. 8-12 w); (2) LEED; (3) pull back rate. We found that for power, estimate=0.746 and p=0.049, for LEED, estimate=0.0638 and p=0.006, and for pull back rate, estimate=0.0378 and p=0.666; implying that power and LEED were significant predictors of successful maintained vein occlusion, but pull back rate had no effect. Figure 3, presents the success probability plots based on univariate logistic regression models. We established cutoff values for power and LEED that yield estimates of 90% probability of success. These results suggest a 90% or greater probability of success for power output> 10.34W (estimate=0.438; p<0.00001) and LEED> 26.56 J/cm (estimate=0.0771; p=0.0008). In terms of univariate logistic regression modeling, the power output was the most important variable determining success (z=4.81, p<0.0000015), this remained true even after subdivision into power groups: [6-7W versus 8-12W] (z=3.10, p<0.002). LEED was the second most influential in determining EVLA success (z=3.35, p<0.0008). Pull back rate remained insignificant in predicting success (p=0.38). Multivariate logistic regression analysis confirmed both power (p<0.040) and LEED (p<0.0084) as significant determinants of ablation success but not the pull back rate, which remained insignificant (p=0.69). The incidence of clinical side effects for up to 1 month post ablation, (defined as DVT confirmed within 10 days post ablation by ultrasound, bruising, severe pain requiring sustained analgesic therapy, paresthesia or numbness, sustained edema requiring diuretic therapy), were all assessed by univariate logistic regression. We found these to be not significantly associated with power output (p=0.14), LEED (p=0.71), or pull back rate (p=0.39). Conclusions: 1) Power and LEED are KEY determinants of long term success of laser saphenous vein ablation. Laser power output is the foremost determinant of ablation success followed by LEED. 2) Pull back rate has an insignificant effect on long term success across the range of pull back rates utilized. 3) A 90% or better probability of long term ablation success is achieved when a power output of>10.34 w and/or a LEED>26.56 J/cm is utilized. 4) Neither power, LEED, or pull back rates were correlated with side effects/complications across the range of values studied. 5) While ideal values for power/LEED will vary according to laser generator and fiber type, as well as clinical population/vein type studied; our findings suggest that utilization of higher power outputs and LEED values commensurate with minimizing side effects, will yield the best results. 6) Limitations: Non-utilization of motorized pull back device (pull back consistency) and non randomized power output assignment. 7) Future randomized studies could test laser/fiber combinations using differing power and motorized pull back combinations that fix LEED at >20 J/cm to confirm exact roles of high vs. low power and pull back velocity in EVLA closure success.
