N2  - Introduction Biliopancreatic diversion changes the normal process of digestion by making the stomach smaller; this allows the ingested food to bypass a part of the small intestine and leads to a smaller amount of food calories absorption. This type of surgery is burdened by serious health risks (even death) and it's indicated and reserved for people with severe obesity (BMI ≥50) who haven't been able to lose weight in any other way. In addition, it's currently established as a primary contraindication for bariatric surgery the coexistence of any kind of eating disorder (specific or NAS). Simplifying the post-operatory phase, the biliopancreatic diversion reduces the original size of the stomach, with consensual reduced amount of eaten food; furthermore, bypassing part of the intestine also means a lowering calories absorption, leading to a weight loss. Others risks (common to all type of bariatric surgeries) include infection of the surgical access, leak of the surgical anastomosys (resulting in peritonitis) and systemic complication such as deep vein thrombosis, or pulmonary embolism, gallstones or nutritional deficiency condition such as anaemia, vitamin deficit or neurological complication. The specific short-term and long-term risks of biliopancreatic diversion, including the dumping syndrome (that causes nausea, weakness, sweating, faintness, and possibly diarrhoea soon after eating; the symptoms get worse after eating highly refined, high-calorie food), osteoporosis, bad smelling stools and diarrhoea (that also lead to poor absorption of protein, fat, calcium, iron, and vitamins B12, A, D, E, and K). There also are a few reports of demise due to unrecognised-untreated proteic malnutrition. If the complication and nutrition deficiency are unmanageable, it's assessable a surgical revision, even burdened by a huge amount of complication and clinical risk. Material & Methods Female, 50 yr. Positive family history for diabetes and scleroderma. Personal diagnosis of bipolar disorder, eating disorder and border-line personality. BMI 35. In 1998 submits a vertical banded gastroplasty (VBG-McLean version) and in 2006 undergo a biliopancreatic diversion. Both post operatory periods are complicated by severe anaemia, with need of hospitalisation and iv support. In the following years, fluctuating BMI and poor adherence to the various treatment required for the several health complications, both clinical and psychiatric. Meanwhile, two-term pregnancies, complicated by malnutrition and anaemia. Persistence of disordered eating behaviour, with nocturnal eating syndrome, binge eating and prandial overeating; subsequently occurrence of nocturnal unconscious eating, with tendency to underweight. For the late appearance of a bleeding gastric ulcer (unresponsive to medical therapy with IPP and endoscopic infiltration), caused by the protrusion of VBG staples, after an evaluation of the high risks related to surgery, the patient undergoes a revision of the biliopancreatic diversion and VBG. Discussion Currently, slow clinical recovery. The patient complains of intense abdominal pain, but the hypos are partly resolved and the BMI is within the normal weight range. Conclusion This case-report highlights once more (if necessary) the need of an adequate clinical and psychiatric evaluation of patients eligible for bariatric surgery, and allows us to observe the anatomical and clinical rehabilitation secondary to gastro-jejunal and pancreatic surgical ri-conversion, after a long period of induced malabsorption.
