N2  - Background: Rivaroxaban is an orally active direct Factor Xa inhibitor and may be used in patients with venous thromboembolism (VTE). Objective: To evaluate tolerance, recurrence, bleeding and adverse events in outpatients VTE that were undergoing treatment with rivaroxaban. Methods: Prospective study from December 2011 to November 2013. Inclusion criteria: (1) Above 18 years old, (2) Diagnosis of pulmonary embolism (PE) or Deep vein thrombosis (DVT). Exclusion criteria: (1) creatinine clearance <30 mL/min; (2) Pregnancy. Selected patients were treated according to clinical criteria and/or their preferences about it. An evaluation of tolerance, side effects (bleeding, recurrences and deaths) and adverse events was also carried out. Results: A total of 103 patients were treated with rivaroxaban, with a mean age of 58±17 (Mean ± standard deviation), and 56% male. Associated comorbidities were: hypertension (30%), diabetes mellitus (10.7%), dyslipidaemia (23.3%), heart disease (10.7%), respiratory disease (25.2%), and history of previous bleeding (7.8%). There was DVT (64.1%), EP (18.4%) and DVT+PE (17.5%). Rivaroxaban was initial treatment in 30% of patients. In 70% rivaroxaban was initiated after low molecular weight heparin (56%) or vitamin K antagonist (43%) whose the median time of anticoagulant previous rivaroxaban was 16 months [range: 1-124 months]. The median time of rivaroxaban treatment was 5 months [range: 21 months]. There were no deaths or recurrences. There were 6 bleeding, one major (acute hemoperitoneum). Adverse events were 6 (5.8%): headache, malaise, gastric intolerance, dizziness, pruritus, diarrhoea syndrome. We had to withdraw treatment in one patient. Conclusions: In our study, rivaroxaban efficacy and safety is consistent with the published randomized clinical trials. The convenience of the dosage, the absence of requirements for periodic controls and few interactions with another drugs regarding VKA requires us to consider this drug in selected group of VTE.
