N2  - Background: Antithrombin deficiency (AT III) is an important risk factors for venous thromboembolism (VTE). The frequency in the thrombophilia patient population is reported to vary between 1% and 8%. Inherited deficiency and acquired disorders can cause low level of AT III. A few patients on prophylactic anticoagulation for immobilization have been observed to develop deep vein thrombosis while still on therapy therefore this study was carried out to bridge the gap in the knowledge of the thrombin activity in VTE patients. Methods: Fifty consecutive patients confirmed to have VTE and 69 controls drawn from the general population were recruited. Platelet poor plasma was obtained from blood collected from participants into a 3.8% sodium citrate bottle at a ratio of 0.1/0.9 (vol/vol) anticoagulant to blood. Antithrombin activity was measured using TECHrom (anti-Xa) kit. Results: The patients mean age was 58.2 years (range 22 to 88 years) with a male: female ratio 1:1.6. Mean±SD for AT III activity of the patients was 74±43.4% while that of control was 90.0±47.6% (p = 0.06). There was insignificant positive correlation between AT III and age of the patients. Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) occurred in 88%, pulmonary embolism (PE) (4%) and other unusual site of VTE was diagnosed in 8%. The mean AT III activity in patients with DVT was 77.8% compared to 49.8% for other forms of VTE. Risk factors for development of VTE were immobility, cancer, diabetes mellitus, obesity, pregnancy and others. The AT III activity was highest in cancer patients (114.2%) and lowest in patients with PE, VTE in unusual site and other causes (p = 0.044). Two patients who developed DVT while on prophylactic anticoagulation had AT III activity of 25%. Thirty-eight percent of the patients had AT III activity less than 60% compared with 20% of the controls (p = 0.04). Eighteen percent of cases compared to 7.2% of controls had AT III activity less than 30% (p = 0.06). Conclusions: Antithrombin activity is not significantly lower in patients with VTE but patients with AT III activity lower than 60% are more likely to have VTE. Antithrombin deficiency may be a more important risk for PE and VTE in unusual site than for deep vein thrombosis.
