N2  - Background: Venous thromboembolism associates DVT and pulmonary embolism (PE). It is a common clinical situation, causing too much morbidity and even mortality. Methods: It is a retrospective study in a department of internal medicine, including records of 100 patients hospitalized for DVT diagnosed by Doppler ultrasound between 2004 and 2013. We report epidemiological, etiological and topographical characteristics of DVT. Results: There were 54 men and 46 women (sex ratio = 1.17), the mean age was 50.7 years (23-84 years), with 21% of patients over 65 years. As medical history, it was found: diabetes (16% of cases), venous thrombosis (10% of cases), neoplasia and renal failure (7% of cases each), recurrent miscarriages (4% of cases), varicose veins operated (3% of cases) and quadriplegia (1% of cases). Obesity with a BMI >30 was noted in 25.4% of cases (average BMI = 26.9 kg/m2). Bed rest (>3 days in the 3 months prior DVT) was noted in 34% of cases. Surgery performed (>a month before the occurrence of DVT) was noted in 7%. Oral contraceptives were found in 13% of patients. A family history of thrombosis was observed in 5% of cases. Pregnancy was noted in three patients. The notion of travel more than 6 hours was found in 3% of these patients. Varicose veins of the lower limbs were found in 33% of cases. DVT were proximal in 62% of cases, 38% were distal. The siege of the DVT was: the lower limbs (97% of cases) (bilateral in one case), the superior vena cava, inferior vena cava and right innominate venous trunk (1% of cases each). The causes of DVT were: paraneoplasic (16%), varicose veins of the limbs (12%), Post traumatic (11%), after confinement to bed (10%), in the context of sepsis (6%), thrombosis of catheter of hemodialysis (6%), following the use of contraceptive pills (6%), during pregnancy or postpartum (5%), postoperative (5%), Behc¸et disease (4%), rheumatoid arthritis, nephrotic syndrome and hyper-homocysteine (1% each). In 14 patients, we didn't found etiology of DVT. Nine of all the DVT were complicated by PE, two of which were serious. Patients were treated by heparin followed by oral anticoagulant for an average of 8 months. The evolution was characterized by the occurrence of bleeding event in 12% of cases, including two gastrointestinal bleeding. We noted four deaths in the aftermath of DVT. Conclusions: Despite advances in antithrombotic prophylaxis, DVT remains a public health problem.
