N2  - Introduction The most recent confidential enquiry reports cardiac disease as the cause of almost 20% of maternal deaths. Myocardial Infarction (MI) is uncommon with an estimated incidence of 0.7 per 100 000 maternities. Independent risk factors for maternal acute MI include advanced maternal age, chronic hypertension, pre-eclampsia and eclampsia. Pregnancy itself confers a 3-4 fold higher risk than non-pregnant women when age matched. Case report A 32-year-old primigravida with no significant medical history booked with BMI of 40.9. She smoked 1/4 oz tobacco daily. Family history included type 2 diabetes, hypertension and thrombosis. Labour was induced at term + 12 and augmented by syntocinon. A live female was delivered normally 12 h later. Glucose tolerance testing at 26/40 was normal. She was risk assessed for venous thrombosis antenatally and did not require antenatal anticoagulants. Two hours postdelivery she complained of tingling in both arms and upper back pain. Blood pressure was raised at 166/99; 200 mg of labetolol was given. Eight hours later she described left anterior chest heaviness radiating through to her back and a restless feeling in her arms. She felt hot and short of breath; observations were normal. Examination was normal, calves soft and not tender. ECG showed right bundle branch block, prominent R wave in V1 and T wave inversion in V1 and V2. Troponin I measurement and CTPA were arranged. Troponin I was significantly raised at 18.2 so full acute coronary syndrome treatment was initiated. CTPA showed no evidence of pulmonary embolism therefore transfer to a tertiary centre for coronary angiography was arranged. Angiography showed a large coronary artery thrombosis which was incompletely removed during the procedure. She was discharged 4 days later on secondary prophylaxis for coronary artery disease and with cardiac rehabilitation. Discussion Our patient had two personal cardiovascular risk factors; high BMI and smoking. There was a family history of hypertension. The diagnosis was unexpected given her vague initial symptoms and low level of suspicion for cardiac events in young women. This case is interesting given the rise in the number of bookings with raised BMI and advanced age. What effect will our changing maternal population have on antenatal resources in the future and can interventions such as the 'healthy eating and lifestyle in pregnancy' (HELP) study make a difference to maternal health.
