N2  - BACKGROUND: Patients with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) have an increased risk for thromboembolism including deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE). Tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α), an inflammatory mediator involved in the pathogenesis of IBD, promotes thromboembolism through several mechanisms, and TNF- α inhibitors could theoretically decrease this risk. The purpose of this study was to identify risk factors for thromboembolism in hospitalized IBD patients and determine if treatment with TNF- α inhibitors was associated with decreased risk. METHODS: We identified IBD patients aged 18-70 hospitalized between July 2002 and July 2011 at our institution. Patients with known malignancy, hypercoagulable state (Factor V Leiden, Antithrombin III deficiency, Protein S or C deficiency), or pregnancy at the time of hospitalization were excluded. Data on age, gender, comorbidities, disease distribution, surgeries, smoking history, medications, reason for hospitalization, and thromboembolism were obtained by chart review. Wald tests were used to calculate association between clinical characteristics and risk of thromboembolism, and multivariate regression was used to develop a model for predicting risk. RESULTS: 547 patients (1048 hospitalizations) were identified. 364 (67%) had Crohn's disease and 183 (33%) had ulcerative colitis (UC). The number of thromboembolic events (DVT, PE, portal vein thrombosis, renal vein thrombosis, and arterial occlusion) was 50. Average age of patients who developed thromboembolism was 51.8 compared to 40.7 in those who did not (P < 0.0001). Associated comorbidities were chronic kidney disease (P = 0.001), diabetes (P = 0.009), and liver disease (P = 0.005). Of reasons for hospitalization, trauma (P = 0.009) and infection (P = 0.009) were most associated with thromboembolism, followed by IBD flare or complication (P = 0.024). Recent hospitalization within the past month (P < 0.001) and prolonged hospitalization greater than 14 days (P < 0.001) were associated with increased risk of thromboembolism. Prednisone was associated with increased risk of thromboembolism (P = 0.003), whereas TNF-α inhibitors were protective (P = 0.011). Immunomodulators were not associated with thromboembolism (P = 0.613). This data was used to develop a model for predicting risk of thromboembolism in IBD. DISCUSSION: In hospitalized IBD patients included in our study, steroid use was associated with an increased risk of thromboembolism, TNF-α inhibitors were protective, and immunomodulators did not affect the risk. Using the model developed in this study, we may better identify patients at increased risk for thromboembolism.
