N2  - OBJECTIVE: The objective of this study was to evaluate the medical and obstetrical outcomes of pregnancy in women aged 50 and older. STUDY DESIGN: The Nationwide Inpatient Sample for the years 2008-2009 was queried for all pregnancy-related discharges. Women aged equal to or greater than 50 and women less than 35 were identified. The prevalence of pre-existing medical conditions and the incidence of medical and obstetric complications during admission for delivery were compared between the two groups. RESULTS: From 2008-2009, there were 880 deliveries to women aged 50 or older and 7,327,077 deliveries to women aged less than 35. The mean ages (± standard deviation) of the older and younger groups were 51.5 (1.5) and 25.8 (4.8), respectively. The cesarean delivery rate for the older group was 61.1 % vs. 30.4 % for the younger group (p<0.001). 18% of the older group had a multiple gestation vs. 1.9% of the younger group (OR 11.9, 95% CI 10.0, 14.1). Women 50 and older were more likely to have chronic hypertension (OR 3.9, 95% CI 3.4,4.5); cardiac arrhythmias and conduction disorders (OR 4.9,95% CI 3.2, 7.0); diabetes (OR 6.2, 95% CI 4.5, 8.3); anemia (OR 1.4, 95% CI 1.2,1.7); thrombocytopenia (OR 3.6, 95% CI 2.3, 5.3); andthrom-bophilias (OR 6.1, 95% CI 4.0, 8.9) compared to younger women. After controlling for multiple gestation during the delivery admission, older women had higher odds for acute heart failure (adjusted OR [aOR] 30.2,95% CI 15.3,53.0), deep vein thrombosis (aOR20.3, 95% CI 9.9, 36.5), and acute renal failure (aOR 21.1, 95% CI 11.0, 36.3). Obstetric complications among women 50 and older are presented in the table below (Table). CONCLUSION: Pregnant women aged 50 and older experience significantly more medical and obstetric complications at the time of a delivery admission compared to women less then 35 years of age. (Table Presented).
