N2  - Background: Pregnancy confers significant risk of venous thromboembolism (VTE), a major cause of maternal morbidity and mortality. Pregnancy complicates diagnosis and treatment of VTE due to concerns about radiation and contrast exposure, bleeding and anticoagulant dosing. Aims: To investigate diagnostic strategy, treatment, and risk factors for VTE during pregnancy and the puerperium. Methods: We conducted a case-control trial of 714 pregnant patients between 1999 and 2009 at Campbelltown Hospital, Sydney. Cases were identifi ed using hospital diagnostic codes for VTE and pregnancy, and confi rmed by review of clinical record and diagnostic testing. Each case was allocated 20 controls, matched by delivery date. We compiled a database pertaining to demographics, diagnosis, comorbidities and treatments. Multivariate logistic regression models were used for case-control comparisons. Findings: Thirty-four VTE diagnoses were confi rmed: 10 pulmonary embolism (PE) and 24 deep venous thrombosis. VTE events were evenly divided between pregnancy and the puerperium, with incidence increasing as pregnancy advanced. For pulmonary embolism, ventilation perfusion scan was the preferred method of diagnosis during pregnancy, generally performed without concurrent chest x-ray. Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) was the preferred anticoagulant during pregnancy, with transition to Warfarin during the puerperium. Both pre-pregnant weight and weight at diagnosis were used in LMWH dose calculation. The tendency was to underdose regardless of the dosing algorithm used. Caesarean-section (OR 2.85; 95% CI, 1.29, 6.32) and previous VTE (OR 10.26; 95% CI, 2.03, 51.92) were associated with significantly increased risk of VTE. Case patients were also significantly older than control patients (mean age 31.5 vs 27.6 respectively; p < 0.05). Weight, hypertension, diabetes and smoking were not associated with increased risk. Conclusion: Imaging investigations in pregnancy may sacrifice diagnostic accuracy to limit radiation exposure. Weight-based dosing used for LMWH is not consistently applied and may compromise therapeutic efficacy. Careful risk-benefi t analysis is required to safely diagnose and treat VTE during pregnancy.
