N2  - Study Objective: To study the efficacy of LNG-IUS in women with abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) and uterine fibroids. Design: IRB approved retrospective cohort (II-2). Setting: University-affiliated teaching hospital. Patients: 62 women with AUB, uterine fibroids and various comorbidities. Intervention: From Jan. 2002-Feb.2007, women with AUB and uterine fibroids were treated with LNG-IUS. Many had comorbidities (83%): 22- obesity, 5-anticoagulated (4 deep vein thromboses, 1 stroke), 5-diabetic, 6-hypertensive, 2-endometrial hyperplasia (1 simple, 1 complex/atypia), 2-asthmatic, and 1 each with multiple sclerosis, heart disease, migraines, thalassemia, ileostomy, renal disease, and rheumatoid arthritis. Ruta Menorrhagia Severity Scores were documented at baseline, and 3, 6, 12, 24 months to determine change in menstrual flow and quality of life. Pelvic ultrasounds were conducted at baseline, 12 and 24 months to determine change in fibroid volume. Measurements and Main Results: The LNG-IUSwas expelled in 5 (8.1%), and removed in 14 (22.6%) women for various reasons; bleeding (7), pain (3), unknown (2), discharge (1), trying for pregnancy (1), One endometrial biopsy indicated endometrioid adenocarcinoma and the patient underwent hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy. Mean Ruta scores decreased in all patients with a 61% and 71% reduction at 1 and 2 years, respectively. Data for fibroid volumes at 1-2 yearswere available for 31 (50.0%) patients. Reduction in dominant fibroid volumewas noted in 19 (61.2%)women with average reduction of -62.6%. Increase in fibroid volume was noted in 9 (29.0%) women with a wide variation in growth, while 3 (9.6%) had no change in fibroid volume (+/-10%). There was no difference in age, body mass index, or parity between fibroid responders and non-responders. Conclusion: LNG-IUS is an effective, minimally invasive method of managing AUB in the presence of uterine fibroids in the majority of high risk women.
