N2  - Purpose: Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) is an acquired hypercoagulable state which presents clinically with arterial or venous thrombosis, or recurrent pregnancy losses. Immunologically, patients have presence of the lupus anticoagulant and/or anti-cardiolipin antibodies. Our goal is to investigate the clinical risk factors and characteristics of gastrointestinal ischemia in patients with APS. Methods: A retrospective chart review was performed and we identified 53 adult patients with antiphospholipid syndrome who were admitted for gastrointestinal ischemia from July 2004 to December 2010. Our primary endpoint was development of gastrointestinal ischemia (including acute or chronic mesenteric ischemia and ischemic colitis). We also investigated demographic, clinical and immunologic data from these patients. Results: In total, 53 patients with a mean age of 60 years (range 18-92; median age 61 years) were identified at our institution. There were 42 females (77%). In terms of social habits, 21 (40%) were smokers and 12 (23%) drank more than one alcoholic beverage per day. Diabetes mellitus was reported in 16 (30%) of patients, hypertension in 31 (58%) and hyperlipidemia in 21 (40%). As far as previous clinical manifestations of APS, 29 (55%) had at least one venous thrombotic event, most commonly deep venous thrombosis or pulmonary embolism, and nine (17%) had a history of an arterial thrombotic event, including stroke. Forty-five (85%) of the APS patients with gastrointestinal ischemia presented with hematochezia while six (11%) had melena. Abdominal pain was present in 11 (21%) of the patients on admission. In terms of laboratory findings, 29 (55%) had anti-cardiolipin antibodies, 42 (79%) had positive lupus anticoagulant, and 13 (25%) had both. Anticoagulation, usually with warfarin, was the most common treatment and was seen in 92% of patients. Conclusion: In patients with APS, gastrointestinal ischemia although rare, is an important complication. A significant number of patients with gastrointestinal ischemia had diabetes, hypertension and hyperlipidemia, but this is most likely related to lifestyle choices and obesity rather than the presence of APS. Hematochezia was the most common symptom, present in 85% of our patients. Overall, in patients presenting with mesenteric thrombosis or ischemic colitis without clear predisposing factors, APS should be ruled out.
