N2  - Cerebral venous and sinus thrombosis (CVST) accounts for less than 1% of all cerebral strokes (CS). However, their frequency is much higher due to the difficulties in establishing a firm diagnosis. According to the World Health Statistics 2008, nearly 40,000 women more than men, die of stroke each year and this gender disparity more significantly is observed in European countries. Awareness of gender differences in manifestation of CVST provided us to investigate a cohort of 78 patients (in equal gender proportion) aged 18 to 77 years with CVST from 1995- 2010. CVST was diagnosed by CT venography and MRI angiography. The risk ratio (RR) and confidence intervals (CI) were estimated using Poisson regression model. The most frequent risk factors in women were found - pregnancy and postpartum period. CVST manifestation was observed during the last trimester of pregnancy and in early puerperium with high age-adjusted RR: 2.23 and 9.70 with 95% CI 0.32 - 1.48 (in 17-34 years patients) and 1.37 and 4.30 with 95% CI 0.20 - 0.61 (in women of 35-44 years). Other main risk factors of CVST in females were existing cardiovascular disease (p<0.01) and diabetes, whereas in the male population, on the first place were deep venous thrombosis in the lower extremities (RR 8.13 with 95% CI 1.12) and alcohol consumption (p<0.02). Women and men tend to recall different ways of CVST manifestation due to dissimilar risk factors of stroke. Therefore, more research is necessary to maintain gender-specific approach to venous CS prevention and treatment.
