N2  - INTRODUCTION: Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE), collectively known as venous thromboembolism (VTE), negatively impact quality of life and incur significant healthcare costs. Mortality from VTE is estimated to be approximately 300,000 deaths in the United States annually. Thus, it is imperative to understand the risk factors for VTE so that the appropriate preventative measures may be taken. Risk factors for VTE were studied in patients who have undergone mastectomy. METHODS: Patients who underwent mastectomies in the American College of Surgeons' National Surgical Quality Improvement Program (ACS-NSQIP) database from 2005-2009 were identified. Univariate analyses were performed to determine associations of DVT and/or PE with each of the following risk factors: age, race, BMI, diabetes, smoking, pregnancy, CHF, hypertension, COPD, inpatient/outpatient status, chemotherapy, radiotherapy, venous catheterization, operative time, and reconstruction. Multivariate logistic regression analysis was used to determine adjusted odds ratios (AOR) and 95% confidence intervals (CI) for risk factors with a p<0.05. RESULTS: Of the 49,028 mastectomy cases identified, 114 patients (0.23%;95%CI:0.19%-0.27%) developed VTE. Of those, 67 had DVT alone, 37 had PE alone, and 10 had both DVT and PE. One patient had two DVTs and no PE. Patients who had a BMI>30 compared to those with BMI<30 were twice as likely (AOR:2.02;95%CI:1.4-2.9) to develop VTE. Similarly, having inpatient status (AOR:2.36;95%CI:1.5-3.8), concomitant venous catheterization (AOR:2.2;95%CI:1.0-4.8), and operations equal to or greater than 3 hours (AOR:2.42;95%CI:1.5- 3.9) increased risk over 2 fold. CONCLUSIONS: Obesity, inpatient status, venous catheterization, and prolonged operative time are risk factors for VTE.
