N2  - Objective: Although assisted reproductive technology (ART) is effective for management of infertility and is widely practiced in many countries in the world, little number of studies published about complications of ART. The study was conducted to estimate the prevalence of complications among 1400 intra-cytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) cycles at Azhart unit. Design: Retrospective study. Material and Methods: This is a retrospective study which includes infertile patients who underwent 1400 ICSI cycles, at Azhart unit, international Islamic center for population studies and research (IICPSR), Al - Azhar university, Egypt. From March 2004 to September 2007. The data were collected by, review follow up sheet and telephone contact. Result: Complications of the procedure developed in 100 cases (7.14%) ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS) 28 cases (2%), vaginal bleeding 65 cases (4.64%), allergic reaction 3 cases (0.21%), pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) 2 cases (0.14%), deep venous thrombosis (DVT) 1 case (0.07%), maternal mortality 1 case (0.07). Pregnancy complications among 210 pregnancies achieved by ICSI, were abortion 59 cases (28.95%), ectopic pregnancy 2 cases (0.95%), multiple pregnancy 47 cases (22.38%), preterm labour 29 cases (13.80%), intrauterine fetal death 3 cases (1.42%), antepartum haemorrhage 5 cases (2.38%), diabetes mellitus 3 cases (1.42%), pregnancy induced hypertension 9 cases (4.28%), premature rupture of membranes 4 cases (1.90%), oligohydraminos 1 case (0.47%) and congenital fetal maleformation 4 cases (1.90%). Pregnancy rate/embryo transfer was 22.87%. Conclusion: At every stage of the procedure there is potential for complication, some of which may be life threatening. When this line of treatment is offered, the indications should be definitive. Counseling of the patients, choice of protocol, monitoring of the patient should be done carefully and must be precious at every step in ART to minimize complications.
