N2  - Aim: To compare the characteristics of the patients (pts) with isolated superficial thrombophlebitis (ST) versus (vs.) pts with ST complicated by deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and/or pulmonary embolism (PE). Methods: 68 pts (59.1 ± 14.4 years, 75% women) with ST were examined. We performed compression ultrasound and thrombophilia workup. We compared the differences between the groups using T-test and Fisher's exact test. Results: The characteristics of the whole group: Body mass index 30.6 ± 7.4, Diabetes mellitus 11.8% pts, Hypertension 55.9%, Hyperlipidaemia 57.4%, Smokers 14.7%, ST on varicose veins 94.1%, Positive family history of ST 27.9%; that of DVT/PE 27.9%, Positive personal history of ST 55.9%; that of DVT/PE 23.1%, Provoked event 35.3% (injury, inflammation, immobilization, hormones, stripping, sclerotherapy, pregnancy, long travel, cancer), Laterality of ST 48.5% right; 47.1% left leg; 4.4% bilateral. Location 22.1% proximal; 39.2% distal; 39.7% both Thrombophilia: factor V Leiden (FVL) 26.5%; mutation in prothrombin gene G20210A 2.9%; protein C deficiency 1.5%; protein S deficiency 1.5%; antiphospholipid syndrome 1.5%; high factor VIII (> 230%) 26.5.%, hyperhomocysteinaemia (> 15 μmol/L) 29.4%. The significant differences between pts with isolated ST (n = 39) vs. ST complicated by DVT/PE (n = 29): male sex (12.8% vs. 41.4%, P = 0.016). FVL (15.4% vs. 41.4%, P = 0.025), unprovoked events (76.9% vs. 48.3%, P = 0.021). In all the other factors, there were no significant differences. Conclusion: Of 68 pts with ST, 29 had concomitant DVT/PE. In this subgroup, there were significantly more men, higher prevalence of FVL and less unprovoked events. Male sex and FVL may be risk factors for the extension of ST to deep vein system.
