C3  - NUVARING
N2  - The two main objectives of adolescence contraception are the eviction of involuntary pregnancies and the prevention of sexually transmitted infections. In France, in spite of our rich contraceptive arsenal and a widely spread information, the rate of voluntary termination of pregnancies keeps growing among the teenagers population. Probably because the gap between theoretical effectiveness and practice of contraception is particularly wide among the young people. Every contraceptive means can be used by teenagers, the best option seems to be the "double DUTCH" which consists of concomitant use of condoms and hormonal contraception. Most often, the consultation for contraception is the first gynaecological consultation. That's the reason why it's usually stressful for teenagers who dread undergoing a gynaecological examination. If this examination is not necessary for most of young patients, it is essential to create a climate of confidence and to make explicit the several contraceptive terms. During this consultation it's interesting to look for common teenage troubles like addiction to smoking and eating disorders. For any prescription of hormonal contraception, it is important to explain the benefits and the possible side effects, to stress the observance and to tell the teenager about the recommendations in case of oversight. With regard to condom, the only efficient way of preventing sexually transmitted infections, it is useful to talk about it in concrete terms, to show its handling and to inform about risks of tearing. With this mind, an emergency contraception can be prescribed straightaway in order to make its use easier. Without moralizing speech, emphasize maturity, taking care of one's own body with the aim of avoiding a premature pregnancy or any sexually transmitted infection. This consultation must be coupled with a close follow-up, availability and share confidence which are the main elements vouching for a good observance and consequently an effectiveness contraception. © Editions ESKA, 2008.
